# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: iderunboardlive
# board: only
# A program runs on the board while the board still answers.
#
# A board carrying the overlay has a stream for its interpreter beside
# its console, so a run does not take its shell: the namespace is read
# HERE, in the middle of a program, which a run that held the shell
# could not allow.  The lamp is what makes the reading mean something --
# the program lights it on its first line and puts it out on its last,
# so a lit lamp read from the namespace is a program still going.
wait 60
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Programming
wait 4
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 150
expect-title Project
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
expect-title Open
click 400 220
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
expect-no-title Open
click-window Project
wait 8
note quiet.bas first, to put the lamp out whatever an earlier run left
key Down
wait 6
key Down
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Run on tiku
wait 200
expect-read /devices/tiku/dev/led0 0
note now the long one, which lights the lamp and then runs on
click-window Project
wait 8
key Up
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Run on tiku
wait 120
note THE reading this script exists for: the board answers its namespace
note while a program of its own is running.  A run that held the board's
note shell -- which is how a board without this stream is reached -- could
note not answer at all, and that is the whole difference.
expect-read /devices/tiku/sys/device/name tiku
note and it IS a program still running that was read past, which is why
note there is a Stop to be offered
click-window Project
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
expect-menu Stop
menu-pick Stop
wait 150
note and stopping it is still a stop: the board is its own again, its
note shell answering as it did before any of this
expect-read /devices/tiku/sys/device/name tiku
quit
